  duplicated. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets a [38;5;153m<script src="src/logger.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  [1mThe API[0m, identical in both runtimes:

  log.[33merror[39m(event, data)   [32m// failures[39m
  log.[33mwarn[39m(event, data)
  log.[33minfo[39m(event, data)    [32m// the behavioral trail[39m
  log.[33mdebug[39m(event, data)   [32m// off by default in production[39m

  [38;5;153mevent[39m is a short stable string ([38;5;153m"login.attempt"[39m, [38;5;153m"login.failed"[39m, [38;5;153m"form.validation_rejected"[39m), [38;5;153mdata[39m an optional object.
  Stable event names are what make a trail greppable later; free-text messages are not.

  [1mOutput[0m is a single structured record per call — timestamp, level, event, runtime, redacted data — [38;5;153mJSON.stringify[39m'd to
  [38;5;153mconsole[level][39m. JSON on both sides because the Node stdout is machine-readable that way, and the browser records are
  then already in the shape a transport would ship.

  [1mThe sink is one pluggable function.[0m [38;5;153mLogger.setSink(fn)[39m defaults to the console writer. That's the seam that makes this
  transport-ready: adding buffer-and-ship later means writing a sink, not touching call sites.

  [1mThe one open choice — does the browser keep a ring buffer now?[0m You asked for a behavioral trail, and console-only
  means the trail evaporates when the tab closes. A ~15-line bounded in-memory buffer (last 50 records, [38;5;153mLogger.dump()[39m to
  retrieve) costs almost nothing, and gives a support person something to ask a user to paste. Without it, the trail
  exists only in a console nobody will read.

  - [1mWith buffer:[0m the trail is actually retrievable today; it's also the exact data structure a future transport ships.
    Cost: 15 lines, and records sit in memory — which raises the stakes on redaction (section 2).
  - [1mWithout buffer:[0m strictly simpler; console-only means genuinely console-only, and the trail half of your goal waits
    for the transport.

  [1mI recommend including it.[0m It's the cheapest part of the whole design and it's the half of your stated goal that
  console output alone doesn't deliver.

  Does this structure look right, and do you want the ring buffer in or out?

